want to evaluate the mean exit time in going over the barrier for analysis of dynamical systems perturbed by small noise. Due to the weakness of the noise, the stochastic trajectory follows the deterministic flow approaching the stable fixed point and then fluctuates within its neighborhood for a long time, if initiating at the attracting domain. Occasionally, it will cross the barrier and finish the exit event. Thus the mean exit time is quite insensitive to the initial point as long as it is far from the boundary. For the Freidlin–Wentzell large deviation theory of the nongradient SDEs with small white noise, the quasipotential is a key concept [15]. In a recent development, there are novel and exciting applications in cardiology, population dynamics, communications, engineering, laser technology, space research, and genetic switches. Aurell and Sneppen [16] examined the problem of escaping from a stable equilibrium and its bifurcation with changing parameter in more than one dimension, and demonstrated how this determines the stability and the robustness of states in genetic networks. Using a nontrivial adjustment of the ordered upwind method (OUM), Cameron computed the quasipotential on a mesh to find the most probable paths based on geometric action [17]. Chen et al. [18] considered the noise-induced escape in an excitable system possessing a quasi-threshold manifold, along which there exists a certain point of minimal quasipotential. Dahiya and Cameron [19] performed numerical computation of the quasipotential for SDEs with multiplicative noise on a mesh, and discussed an application to the Maier–Stein model with anisotropic diffusion. Lv et al. [20] characterized the metastability of gene regulatory system perturbed by intrinsic noise, and constructed the global quasipotential energy landscape to calculate the optimal transition paths between the on and off states based on the large deviation theory. Nolting and Abbott [21] visualized stable states in stochastic systems using a ball-in-cup diagram, and provided the quasipotential as a practical tool to quantify stability in stochastic systems. Yang et al. [22] analyzed the ordered line integral method with midpoint rule for finding the quasipotential in 3D by use of Karush–Kuhn–Tucker theory for rejecting unnecessary simplex updates, and conducted an upgraded hierarchical update strategy to prune the number for admissible simplexes and a fast search of them. A neural network is a powerful parallel information-processing system and a universal approximation map [23]. It is composed of neurons and is trained to search for the minimum of the loss function during the learning process by gradient descent method. Neural networks are being used extensively in the fields of aeronautics, robotics, defense, engineering, telecommunications, manufacturing, medicine, insurance, psychology, banking, security, marketing, and finance [24]. These artificial networks may be used for predictive modeling, adaptive control and applications where they can be trained via a dataset [25]. Self-learning resulting from experience can occur within networks, which can derive conclusions from a complex and seemingly unrelated set of information [26]. It is worth pointing out that machine learning provides a means of tackling nondeterministic systems, where the equations used to model the system are not known [27]. It is meaningful to investigate the mean exit time that describes the escape time of stochastic process from a basin of attraction. Our goal is to control the mean exit time of stochastic dynamical systems to achieve a desired value based on the quasipotential and machine learning. The process of the control strategy visualizes the dynamics, and allows us to find most probable transition paths and estimate stationary probability density. Kularatne et al. [28] presented a limited control strategy, depending on the basin boundaries of the stable equilibrium of the deterministic system. Heckman et al. [29] exhibited the potential of optimal control strategies that work in combination with the endemic stochastic environment. To advance the control strategy, our method requires minimal energy consumption, and the most probable path remains unchanged after adding control. The greatest difficulty stems from the computation of the quasipotential function, especially for high-dimensional systems. We design a neural network architecture to achieve this goal and apply it to a three-dimensional stochastic double-well example successfully for illustration. This paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we make a portrait of the quasipotential as the potential component of orthogonal decomposition for the nongradient SDEs with a finite number of isolated attractors by decomposing a smooth nongradient vector field to a potential component and a rotational component in each basin of attraction. In Section 3, we give a description of the control strategy and devise a new technique to control the mean exit time of stochastic dynamical systems by utilizing the quasipotential and neural network. In Section 4, we perform numerical experiments to demonstrate the effectiveness of our control strategy by computing the quasipotential and the most probable paths in stochastic Maier–Stein model having two distinct stable states. In Section 5, we characterize the quasipotential and mean exit time of three-dimensional double-well system. In Section 6, we draw the conclusions from the discussions and summarize the future challenges.
